<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Deliberately empty, and that is the interesting part.

  This app is NOT sandboxed. It reads the game's log from
  ~/Library/Application Support/Binding of Isaac Afterbirth+/, runs the game's own
  ResourceExtractor out of the Steam folder, and captures the game window. None of that
  is possible in a sandbox without a pile of temporary exceptions, so the absence of
  com.apple.security.app-sandbox is a decision rather than an oversight.

  Under the hardened runtime (Developer ID builds only, see make-app.sh) the app still
  needs no exceptions:
    ScreenCaptureKit is gated by TCC, not by an entitlement.
    Nothing loads third-party plug-ins, so library validation stays on.
    Nothing scripts other apps, so no Apple Events entitlement.
    Nothing JITs, so no allow-jit.

  If a future feature needs one, add it here with a comment naming the feature. An
  unexplained entitlement is a permission nobody can audit later.

  (Note for editors: XML comments may not contain a double hyphen. AMFI rejects the
  whole file with "syntax error" if one sneaks in, and codesign then signs ad-hoc.)
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
